01:21:15,000 --> 01:21:21,000
Reds is a 1981 American epic historical drama film co-written, produced, and directed by Warren Beatty, about the life and career of John
Reed, the journalist and writer who chronicled the October Revolution in Russia in his 1919 book Ten Days That Shook the World.

11
01:29:22,500 --> 01:29:28,500
Beatty stars in the lead role alongside Diane Keaton as activist Louise
Bryant and Jack Nicholson as playwright Eugene O'Neill.

12
01:37:30,000 --> 01:37:36,000
The supporting cast includes Edward Herrmann, Jerzy Kosiński, Paul Sorvino,
Maureen Stapleton, Gene Hackman, Ramon Bieri, Nicolas Coster and M.

13
01:45:37,500 --> 01:45:43,500
Emmet Walsh.

14
01:53:45,000 --> 01:53:51,000
The film also features, as "witnesses", interviews with the 98-year-old radical educator and peace activist Scott Nearing, author Dorothy
Frooks, reporter and author George Seldes, civil liberties advocate Roger Baldwin and writer Henry Miller, among others.

15
02:01:52,500 --> 02:01:58,500
George Jessel, who died shortly before the film was released, appears as one
of the contemporaries of Reed and Bryant who were interviewed.

16
02:10:00,000 --> 02:10:06,000
Reds was released on December 4, 1981, to widespread critical acclaim.

17
02:18:07,500 --> 02:18:13,500
Beatty was awarded the Academy Award for Best Director and the film was
nominated for Best Picture, but lost to Chariots of Fire.

18
02:26:15,000 --> 02:26:21,000
Beatty, Keaton, Nicholson, and Stapleton were nominated for Best Actor, Best Actress, Best Supporting
Actor, and Best Supporting Actress, respectively, with Stapleton winning her category.

19
02:34:22,500 --> 02:34:28,500
Beatty became the third person to be nominated for Academy Awards in the categories Best Director, Actor, and, with co-writer
Trevor Griffiths, Original Screenplay—losing again to Chariots of Fire—for a film nominated for Best Picture.

20
02:42:30,000 --> 02:42:36,000
In June 2008, the American Film Institute revealed AFI's 10 Top 10—the best ten films in ten
"classic" American film genres—after polling over 1,500 people from the film community.

21
02:50:37,500 --> 02:50:43,500
Reds came in ninth in the epic genre.

22
02:58:45,000 --> 02:58:51,000
The film explores themes of husband wife relationship, world war i,
war correspondent, biography, based on true story.

23
03:06:52,500 --> 03:06:58,500
